<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/expert/electrodynamics-maxwell">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Maxwell's four equations are the complete classical theory of electricity, magnetism and
    light — the first great unification in physics. Written out, they are four statements about
    how electric (E) and magnetic (B) fields fill space and respond to charges and each other.
    Their most famous child wasn't even sought: the equations <strong>predict waves travelling
    at exactly the measured speed of light</strong>, revealing that optics was secretly
    electromagnetism.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four laws and what each one says — the chain ends with light itself" widget="equationStep" config={maxwellCfg} />

  <h2>Why this is the template for physics</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Fields are the actors.</strong> Maxwell removed "action at a distance": charges make fields, fields push charges, and changes propagate at finite speed. Every modern force theory (including general relativity and QFT) is written in this field language.</li>
    <li><strong>Symmetry guided the discovery.</strong> Maxwell's fourth equation was added for mathematical consistency — a missing term (the displacement current) that experiments confirmed later. Theory leading experiment, a first in physics.</li>
    <li><strong>The spectrum is one object:</strong> radio, microwaves, infrared, visible, UV, X-rays, gamma — all the same wave, differing only in frequency. Maxwell knew none of them; his equations contained them all. Hertz confirmed radio waves in 1887.</li>
    <li><strong>What's left for quantum theory:</strong> classical electrodynamics cannot explain black-body radiation or the photoelectric effect — the cracks the quantum ladder grew from. The classical theory is exact for fields, wrong about how light comes in packets.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Maxwell, "A Dynamical Theory of the Electromagnetic Field" (1865); Hertz (1887); Feynman Lectures Vol. II.</p>
</TopicPage>
